Kubernetes itself doesn’t have users like Linux. Instead, it uses certificates or service accounts.

👉 Since you want a real user (QA person), we’ll use certificate authentication:

Generate key and CSR for QA user
# Generate private key
openssl genrsa -out qa.key 2048

# Generate CSR
openssl req -new -key qa.key -out qa.csr -subj "/CN=qa-user/O=qa-team"
CN=qa-user → Common Name (username)

O=qa-team → Group name (optional)

🔹 Step 3: Sign Certificate with Kubernetes CA
Kubernetes uses its own CA to trust users.

# Sign CSR with Kubernetes CA (adjust paths for your cluster)
openssl x509 -req -in qa.csr -CA /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
    -CAkey /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.key -CAcreateserial \
    -out qa.crt -days 365
Now you have qa.crt and qa.key for the user.

🔹 Step 4: Configure kubeconfig for QA user
kubectl config set-credentials qa-user \
    --client-certificate=qa.crt \
    --client-key=qa.key \
    --embed-certs=true

kubectl config set-context qa-context \
    --cluster=kubernetes \
    --namespace=qa \
    --user=qa-user
Now QA user has their own kubeconfig context.

