    namespace: qa
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: qa-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
Apply:

kubectl apply -f rolebinding-qa.yaml
6) Issue a token for the ServiceAccount
(Requires Kubernetes >= v1.24 for kubectl create token.)

TOKEN=$(kubectl -n $NAMESPACE create token $SA --duration=$TOKEN_DURATION)
echo "$TOKEN"  # copy this to hand to the user or to embed in a kubeconfig
If your cluster is older and still uses secret-based SA tokens, use:

SECRET_NAME=$(kubectl -n $NAMESPACE get sa $SA -o jsonpath='{.secrets[0].name}')
kubectl -n $NAMESPACE get secret $SECRET_NAME -o go-template='{{.data.token}}' | base64 -d
7) Create a kubeconfig file for the QA person (so they can kubectl from laptop)
Run these commands on a machine that already has admin kubeconfig (we’ll create $OUT_KUBECONFIG):

CURRENT_CTX=$(kubectl config current-context)
CLUSTER_NAME=$(kubectl config view -o jsonpath="{.contexts[?(@.name=='$CURRENT_CTX')].context.cluster}")
CLUSTER_SERVER=$(kubectl config view -o jsonpath="{.clusters[?(@.name=='$CLUSTER_NAME')].cluster.server}")
CA_DATA=$(kubectl config view --raw -o jsonpath="{.clusters[?(@.name=='$CLUSTER_NAME')].cluster.certificate-authority-data}")

# token from step 6
# TOKEN=...

# create cluster entry and embed the CA data
kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG config set-cluster $CLUSTER_NAME \
  --server="$CLUSTER_SERVER" \
  --certificate-authority-data="$CA_DATA" \
  --embed-certs=true

# set token as the user credential
kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG config set-credentials $SA --token="$TOKEN"

# set context with namespace
kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG config set-context $SA-context \
  --cluster="$CLUSTER_NAME" --user="$SA" --namespace="$NAMESPACE"

kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG config use-context $SA-context

# show the file path to hand off
echo "Created kubeconfig: $(realpath $OUT_KUBECONFIG)"
Give that qa-kubeconfig file to your QA user. They can do:

kubectl --kubeconfig=qa-kubeconfig get pods
8) Quick tests (as QA user via the kubeconfig)
# should be allowed
kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG auth can-i list pods -n $NAMESPACE

# should be denied
kubectl --kubeconfig=$OUT_KUBECONFIG auth can-i list pods -n default
9) How to immediately revoke or change access
To remove permissions immediately: delete the RoleBinding:

kubectl -n $NAMESPACE delete rolebinding $ROLEBIND_NAME
RBAC is checked at each API request, so removing RoleBinding will immediately block the token from performing the granted actions (even if token still valid).

To remove the ServiceAccount entirely:

kubectl -n $NAMESPACE delete sa $SA
To shorten access: create tokens with shorter --duration or re-issue a new token and replace the kubeconfig.

10) Notes, best practices & gotchas
kubectl create token issues a short-lived token (default 1h); you can set --duration. Prefer short durations for security.

If you hand out a kubeconfig file with a token, treat it like a password — deliver via secure channel.

Prefer lowest privileges (least privilege principle): only give the verbs/resources the user actually needs.

For long-lived human access in a company, consider OIDC/SSO instead of distributing tokens.

If you inject a token into a Pod manually (Secret + mount), it will not auto-rotate — you must rotate it yourself.

